{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2159 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "2be2d8664b38f5871314ce3a1ce0601100dcc3062d6b9edb17ec7ad233bb93da" :edb/db-before-t 2158 :edb/db-after-t 2159 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535471 50 #inst "2026-10-09T00:45:26.561Z" 13194139535471 true] [17592186152113 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350819 13194139535471 true] [17592186152115 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350823 13194139535471 true] [17592186287310 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065811/assets/5bd3bf7cecf806ab0fc89db59afc5d385064080f47076d13d2ef25650ce39a23.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse shown above?" 13194139535471 true] [17592186287310 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/5bd3bf7cecf806ab0fc89db59afc5d385064080f47076d13d2ef25650ce39a23.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse shown above?" 13194139535471 false] [17592186309970 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065811/assets/6f983fda4328227558e46aaaec454db2ccc0611331495c923ad1d90c7cb3df8a.png)\n\nThe semi-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$]$" 13194139535471 true] [17592186309970 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/assets/6f983fda4328227558e46aaaec454db2ccc0611331495c923ad1d90c7cb3df8a.png)\n\nThe semi-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$]$" 13194139535471 false] [17592186309970 1145 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=8$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a semi-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065811/assets/101d9b26027bc5667009b0c7af69fa190c987c362122402fce62daec5833092f.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\pi ]$, we get the semi-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the semi-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$" 13194139535471 true] [17592186309970 1145 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=8$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a semi-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/assets/101d9b26027bc5667009b0c7af69fa190c987c362122402fce62daec5833092f.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\pi ]$, we get the semi-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the semi-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$" 13194139535471 false] [17592186309996 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065811/assets/5fb18775e625816f119cf95c09051eba41fced187f4e4d1e7964921f4b877288.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse shown above?" 13194139535471 true] [17592186309996 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/assets/5fb18775e625816f119cf95c09051eba41fced187f4e4d1e7964921f4b877288.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse shown above?" 13194139535471 false] [17592186350819 1017 #edb/ref 17592186350820 13194139535471 true] [17592186350819 1089 "q-327809" 13194139535471 true] [17592186350819 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535471 true] [17592186350819 1100 "The Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" 13194139535471 true] [17592186350819 1113 #uuid "0c2b92e3-65f3-50ac-b60f-da322c49e8d0" 13194139535471 true] [17592186350819 1145 "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =9\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{9} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{9}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{81} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{4\\sqrt{3}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{(4\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{48} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{81}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{48}={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{81}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{48}=1.\n$$" 13194139535471 true] [17592186350820 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350821 13194139535471 true] [17592186350820 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350822 13194139535471 true] [17592186350820 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535471 true] [17592186350820 1041 "field-1" 13194139535471 true] [17592186350820 1104 #edb/ref 17592186350822 13194139535471 true] [17592186350820 1110 #uuid "00e0e8ab-f5df-517b-b20c-b0d83df16c90" 13194139535471 true] [17592186350821 1034 "\\frac{x^2}{9}+\\frac{y^2}{48}" 13194139535471 true] [17592186350821 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535471 true] [17592186350821 1111 #uuid "b8d59c10-32e0-5912-b9cf-4e4eaea6e549" 13194139535471 true] [17592186350822 1034 "\\frac{x^2}{81}+\\frac{y^2}{48}" 13194139535471 true] [17592186350822 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535471 true] [17592186350822 1111 #uuid "6965f472-60e1-58d9-b012-8d39b8d0be10" 13194139535471 true] [17592186350823 1017 #edb/ref 17592186350824 13194139535471 true] [17592186350823 1089 "q-79282" 13194139535471 true] [17592186350823 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535471 true] [17592186350823 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065811/assets/1617a8efd9110fa435dc396d90450c7fe17d18105e2358ab5610135e32a385ad.png)\n\nThe half-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" 13194139535471 true] [17592186350823 1113 #uuid "b63b1e1e-81bd-51c3-84ae-72e1137b3714" 13194139535471 true] [17592186350823 1145 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(-3,-1),$ $a=4$ and $b=2.$  Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a half-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the line parallel to the $x$-axis and crossing the center of the ellipse.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065811/assets/4659fda20ed9a2b5e5c10d60f504e4db378efb0bfe9ae037f4998511e75300cb.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\pi ]$, we get the half-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the half-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$" 13194139535471 true] [17592186350824 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350825 13194139535471 true] [17592186350824 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350826 13194139535471 true] [17592186350824 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350827 13194139535471 true] [17592186350824 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350828 13194139535471 true] [17592186350824 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350829 13194139535471 true] [17592186350824 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535471 true] [17592186350824 1041 "selection" 13194139535